\title{The verdict protocol: optimistic environment, pessimistic verdict}
\taxon{Design}
\date{2026-11-23}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{driver}
\tag{soundness}
\meta{status}{implemented}
\meta{created}{2026-11-23}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-25}
\meta{origin}{docs/design/ARCHITECTURE.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} implemented in \code{rlvm-driver} and \code{rlvm-cli}, with the verdict rule of the reconciliation instead of the first draft's. Revised on 2026-11-25, when the default configuration changed (\commit{a8dc6dd}): a rejection of the primary engine is now final in a plain run, and the re-check of a rejection is the protocol of one development option, \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}; see [the decision](dec-PUU0). The sentences of this note that described the re-check as the protocol of every run are marked where they stand.}

\p{RLVM answers accept, reject or decline for a file. A unit is one declaration record of the export; an engine answers accept, reject or decline for a unit. This note is the chain between the two. Its purpose is that no optimisation, cache, schedule or crash can turn into a wrong answer: a wrong answer in either direction outweighs any speed, and a decline is always available.}

\subtree[design-verdict-protocol-statuses]{
\title{Statuses and the fail-closed chain}

\table{
\tr{\th{Exit status}\th{Meaning}}
\tr{\td{0}\td{every unit accepted, or skipped as unsafe or partial; the end of the file was reached and the counts are consistent}}
\tr{\td{1}\td{malformed input, or a rejection: of the primary engine, of the environment pass or of a structural check. Until 2026-11-25 this row read "a rejection confirmed by the reference engine"; that is still what it means with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} and where the reference engine is the primary engine}}
\tr{\td{2}\td{no rejection decides the file, but something was declined}}
\tr{\td{3}\td{internal error: an \code{Error}, a dead thread, a launcher failure}}
\tr{\td{64}\td{usage error}}
}

\p{Three independent defaults must all be overturned for a 0. The launcher's status starts as 3 and takes a verdict only from a check that returned normally. The driver's verdict board says decline until the loader has reported the complete file. And completion yields accept only if every unit is accepted or skipped, the number of units equals the number of declaration records the reader counted, and no unit has another status. Every unit status is written once.}
}

\subtree[design-verdict-protocol-optimistic]{
\title{Optimistic environment}

\p{Units are checked against the \em{statements} of earlier units, not against their verdicts: a worker reads the type of an earlier declaration before anybody has checked it. This is what lets loading, the environment pass and the workers overlap, see [the driver](design-driver). It is sound because the file is accepted only if every unit is. A wrong earlier unit can only make a later check fail, loop or crash, and it yields a non-accept outcome by itself. The price is totality: an engine may be fed the body of an ill-typed earlier definition, so it must end with a decline on garbage, with bounds-checked ids and budgets, and every exception is caught at the unit boundary. Visibility is enforced once per root: a unit of rank r may mention only constants of smaller rank, which is one integer test on the summary \code{depMax} of [the term store](design-term-store).}
}

\subtree[design-verdict-protocol-rule]{
\title{The verdict rule}

\p{The verdict is the status of the first unit in file order that is not accepted or skipped: rejected gives reject, declined or "no status" gives decline. Later units never change it. A rejection is recorded as the primary engine gave it; until 2026-11-25, and still with the development verification of the next section, it was recorded only when it was confirmed. Malformed input gives reject whatever was checked, because the official reader rejects before any checking; unsupported input and I/O errors never give accept. The first draft gave two rules, "reject iff some rejection was confirmed" and "the first failure in file order". The soundness critic of [the design review](design-review-reconciliation) pointed out that a final decline of an early unit cancels later units, so whether a later rejecting unit was already checked depends on timing, and the same bad file could end in reject or decline with different worker counts (finding C3.13). The rule above is deterministic.}
}

\subtree[design-verdict-protocol-recheck]{
\title{The re-check protocol}

\p{\strong{What runs since 2026-11-25.} In a plain run an accept of the primary engine is final, and so is a rejection: the driver records it as the machine gave it, with no strict run and no reference engine. Only the rows "decline" and "exception" of the table below still run in every configuration. They are the \em{decline fallback}: a unit the machine declines is tried again with a cold budget and then decided by [the reference engine](design-reference-engine). The fallback stays until the machine decides those units itself, 95 units of six of the eight corpora, and \code{\verb~|-X fallback=none~} turns it off. The rows of a warm reject are the \em{verification}, a development option: \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} turns them on, together with the launcher's second run under the strict policy for a rejected file with dropped metadata, see [the decision on the metadata class](dec-7UAL); a run whose primary engine is the reference engine (\code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~}, \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~}) or whose oracle it is (\code{\verb~|-X engine=diff~}) verifies unasked. \code{\verb~|-X no-recheck~} is the old spelling of the new default, \code{\verb~|-X verify=none~}. The statistics line of a run names both settings; a plain run says \code{\verb~|verify=none fallback=reference~}. This is the maintainer's instruction of 2026-11-25, that the reference engine is a tool of development and is not to affect production; see [the decision](dec-PUU0) and, for the census behind it, [the report](rep-MO-1).}

\table{
\tr{\th{Run}\th{A rejection of the machine}\th{A unit the machine declines}\th{A rejected file with dropped metadata}}
\tr{\td{plain, also \code{\verb~|-X verify=none~} and \code{\verb~|-X no-recheck~}}\td{final}\td{decided by the reference engine}\td{rejected}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}, the default until 2026-11-25}\td{re-checked: a strict machine, then the reference engine}\td{decided by the reference engine}\td{checked again under the strict policy}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-X fallback=none~}: the machine alone}\td{final}\td{declined}\td{rejected}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-X verify=reference -X fallback=none~}: the gate of an engine under test}\td{confirmed, or declined as a disagreement}\td{declined}\td{checked again under the strict policy}}
}

\p{\strong{The protocol as designed}, which was the protocol of every run until 2026-11-25. An accept of the primary engine is final and is never re-checked: it is the hot path, and its soundness is the engine's own obligation, shown by [the gate](design-gate) and attacked by [the adversarial reviews](design-adversarial-review). Since 2026-11-25 it has a second case, the accept of a repeat on the word of its original, see the next section. Everything else is checked again, at once, on the worker's own thread, in a freshly reset session with a cold budget.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Warm outcome}\th{Stage}\th{Final status}}
\tr{\td{reject}\td{1. the primary engine in strict mode accepts}\td{accepted; logged as WARM-ANOMALY, a cache or shortcut produced a wrong rejection}}
\tr{\td{}\td{2. the reference engine rejects}\td{rejected, confirmed; the reason reported is the reference engine's}}
\tr{\td{}\td{the reference engine accepts}\td{accepted; logged as MACHINE-INCOMPLETE}}
\tr{\td{}\td{the reference engine declines or throws}\td{declined as an unconfirmed rejection, by policy}}
\tr{\td{decline}\td{1. the primary engine with a cold budget accepts}\td{accepted}}
\tr{\td{}\td{2. the reference engine}\td{its accept, reject or decline}}
\tr{\td{exception}\td{session discarded; the reference engine}\td{its outcome; an internal-error decline if it throws too}}
}

\p{Accepting on the reference engine's word after the primary engine rejected is sound: the reference engine is a complete checker in its own right. This step is the rule "shortcuts fail open into the complete official procedure" at the granularity of a declaration, and while it ran by default it was what gave item (c) of [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract): everything the official checker accepts is accepted. When the reference engine is itself the primary engine, one fresh cold run decides. Structural rejections and rejections of the environment pass need no confirmation. A WARM-ANOMALY fails a test build. The gate of an engine under test runs with the verification on and the fallback off, so that the engine must accept every unit by itself.}

\p{\strong{What a final rejection changes.} On real input nothing: the machine rejects no unit of the eight corpora, and of its 1,960 rejections on the 262 small arena tests and the 17,775 mutants the re-check changed none. On the hostile suite of 1,274 rows the re-check changed 16 of 737 rejections: 12 official accepts that the machine rejects, which are now the known wrong rejects of the default configuration, see [accepted risks](accepted-risks-wrong-rejects), and 4 official rejections that a strict machine accepted, which now get the official verdict. So item (c) holds in a plain run on every real input measured and not on those twelve hand-made files. And a rejection that comes out of a warm session depends on what the session checked before, so on such input the verdict depends on the schedule: the driver's invariance suite finds 21 hostile rows with a schedule difference without the re-check and 12 with it, and none on 17,159 files of the published tests and the mutants, see [the driver's invariants](design-driver-invariants) and [the insight](ins-RPOG).}
}

\subtree[design-verdict-protocol-repeats]{
\title{The accept of a repeat}

\p{Since 2026-11-25 a definition, theorem or opaque that repeats an earlier unit word for word (the same kind, the same list of universe-parameter names, the same reducibility hint, and the \em{same type and value records} of the export) is reported accepted without a check when its original is ACCEPTED on the verdict board; its status is accepted, not the status of an unsafe or partial record that is left out. The unit still goes through admission: the environment frontier, its name, its universe parameters, its roots. How a worker proceeds is in [the driver](design-driver-repeats): when the original has no status yet, the repeat is checked like any unit; if that check does not accept, the worker waits for the original's status. The reference engine as primary engine never skips, so under \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~} every declaration is checked, and \code{\verb~|-X repeats=check~} checks every unit in the default configuration too.}

\p{\strong{Why the verdict of a file is unchanged.} The verdict is the status of the first unit in file order that is not accepted. A repeat is accepted on the word of its original only when the original \em{is} accepted, and a status is written once; if the original is rejected or declined, the verdict is decided at or before the original, and the repeat lies above the first failure. So the skip can change a verdict only where the check of the repeat would not have accepted although the check of the original did. No accept of an underivable statement can come of that: the value of the original has its type in the environment of the original, a later environment only adds constants, and the statement of the repeat is the statement of the original. That the second check accepts whenever the first did is a claim about the check. For these three kinds the official kernel reads the kind, the universe parameters, the type and the value, as terms and as objects, and the constants they mention; of the declaration's own name it asks only that it is not declared yet, which admission tests; and one export record is one object. The claim was tested with official verdicts: the official checker gives a repeat the verdict of its original on 1,193 of 1,193 files made from the hostile suite by repeating the last declaration of a row, and in a differential run the reference engine accepts every skipped unit of the eight corpora.}

\p{\strong{The one hostile row.} For the machine the claim holds only up to its own tolerated shortcuts. The file \code{R-e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad} is the row \code{e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad} of the tolerated kind (finding E1-F7, the K rule on a major premise that is a beta-redex) with its theorem repeated. The official checker rejects it; the statement is derivable. The default configuration accepts the original, one of its tolerated accepts, and rejects the same two records when it checks them a second time, a rejection that the reference engine confirmed while the re-check protocol was the default. The machine's two answers differ because the two values live in different places: the proof of the original is a proof block decoded for that unit, the value of the repeat is the promoted copy of it, a shared term of an earlier unit, and the machine's tabling gate distinguishes the two. Without the skip the default configuration therefore rejected this file, at the repeat, which is the official verdict; with the skip it accepts it. That is a tolerated accept under the practical policy; under the strict policy the file is rejected, as officially. No underivable statement is accepted. It is the only verdict that differs between the skip and the check on the 1,648 hostile files made for the question, and it is the reason for the wait: without it the verdict of this file would depend on whether the original was decided before the repeat came up. See [the report](rep-OPT-I), sections 3.2 to 3.5, [the divergence classes](design-official-divergence), [the decision that took it](dec-RHRE) and [the changelog entry](log-YGJO).}
}

\subtree[design-verdict-protocol-resources]{
\title{Resources}

\p{No budget is expressed in time; a timeout would be scored like the decline it tries to avoid. Warm sessions have no step limit; step budgets exist only in strict and differential runs, and the machine has a bound of its own on the work of one unit. A budget that runs out, a depth limit of the reference engine and the memory account over its limit all give a decline, never a rejection. A decline for memory in a warm session is retried one unit at a time before it counts. An \code{Error} on any thread cancels the run and ends the process with status 3.}
}

\p{Documents: [design-architecture-document](design-architecture-document) sections 4.1, 6.5, 6.6 and 8; [design-reconciliation-document](design-reconciliation-document) section 2, "Verdict rule" and "Faults inside TRY"; \src{rlvm-driver/README.md} sections "Re-check protocol" and "Verdict"; \src{rlvm-cli/README.md} for the exit-status protocol. Source: [the design session](ses-EMMW); built in [the reference pipeline](ses-BQZM).}
